As Birmingham were in the [[Football League First Division|First Division]], they entered the competition in the first round. They were drawn against [[Southend United F.C.|Southend United]]. Birmingham won the first leg at Southend's home ground [[Roots Hall]] 5–0, courtesy of goals from [[Nicky Eaden]], [[Marcelo dos Santos Cipriano|Marcelo]], [[Michael Johnson (footballer, born 1973)|Michael Johnson]], [[Dele Adebola]] and [[Bryan Hughes]]. The second leg at Birmingham's home ground, [[St Andrew's (stadium)|St Andrew's]] finished 0–0, with Birmingham progressing to the second round with a 5–0 aggregate victory.{{sfn|Haylett|2001|p=29}} Second Division team [[Wycombe Wanderers F.C.|Wycombe Wanderers]] were the opposition in the second round. Birmingham established a three-goal lead by the 24th minute, courtesy of an [[own goal]] by [[Paul McCarthy (footballer, born 1971)|Paul McCarthy]], with goals from Johnson and striker [[Geoff Horsfield]]. However, by the 86th minute Wycombe had levelled the match with goals from [[Andy Rammell]], [[Andy Baird]] and [[Jamie Bates (footballer)|Jamie Bates]]. Parity was short-lived, as Birmingham scored a minute later through striker [[Andrew Johnson (footballer, born 1981)|Andrew Johnson]] to win the first leg 4–3.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/sport1/hi/football/worthington_cup/930765.stm |title=Wycombe 3–4 Birmingham |publisher=BBC Sport |date=19 September 2000 |access-date=8 September 2015 }}</ref> Birmingham won the second leg at St Andrew's 1–0, courtesy of a [[Peter Ndlovu]] goal to win the tie 5–3 on aggregate.{{sfn|Haylett|2001|p=29}}

Premier League team [[Tottenham Hotspur F.C.|Tottenham Hotspur]] were the opposition in the third round. Birmingham took the lead in the match held at Tottenham's home ground, [[White Hart Lane]], in the 15th minute when Adebola scored. He scored again in the 28th minute and a goal from [[Mark Burchill]] in the 45th minute gave Birmingham a 3–0 lead at half-time. Tottenham pulled a goal back through a [[Penalty kick (association football)|penalty kick]] scored by [[Darren Anderton]], but they were unable to level the match and Birmingham won 3–1.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/worthington_cup/999084.stm |title=Spurs 1–3 Birmingham |publisher=BBC Sport |date=31 October 2000 |access-date=8 September 2015 }}</ref> They faced Premier League team [[Newcastle United F.C.|Newcastle United]] in the fourth round, with the match held at St Andrew's. Newcastle took the lead in the 14th minute when [[Kieron Dyer]] scored, but Birmingham equalised in the 31st minute when Adebola scored. The match remained level until the final minute when Michael Johnson scored to give Birmingham a 2–1 victory.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/worthington_cup/1045312.stm |title=Birmingham topple Newcastle |date=29 November 2000 |access-date=8 September 2015 }}</ref> Fellow First Division team [[Sheffield Wednesday F.C.|Sheffield Wednesday]] were the opposition in the quarter-finals. Birmingham won the match 2–0, courtesy of goals from [[Danny Sonner]] and Adebola.{{sfn|Haylett|2001|p=29}}

Birmingham were drawn against Premier League team [[Ipswich Town F.C.|Ipswich Town]] in the semi-finals. The first leg held at Ipswich's home ground, [[Portman Road]], was won by the home side. A [[Marcus Stewart]] penalty secured a 1–0 victory.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/worthington_cup/1108765.stm |title=Clockwatch: Ipswich 1–0 Birmingham |publisher=BBC Sport |date=9 January 2001 |access-date=8 September 2001 }}</ref> Birmingham took the lead in the second leg at St Andrew's when Horsfield scored in the 43rd minute. He scored again in the 55th minute, but Ipswich striker [[James Scowcroft]] scored a minute later. No further goals were scored up to full-time with the score at 2–1, but with the tie level at 2–2, it went to extra-time. Goals from [[Martin Grainger]] and Andrew Johnson secured a 4–1 victory and a 4–2 aggregate win to send Birmingham into the final.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/worthington_cup/1144836.stm |title=Birmingham storm into final |publisher=BBC Sport |date=31 January 2001 |access-date=8 September 2015 }}</ref>

